<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Identifying bounds and solving a problem</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p></p><p>\(p = {@aa@} \), correct to 2 significant figures.<br>\(q = {@bb@}&nbsp; \), correct to 2 significant figures.<br>\(r = {@cc@} \), correct to 2 significant figures.</p><p>(i) Find the upper and lower bounds of \(p,q\) and \(r\).</p><p>[[input:ans1]] \(  \leq p&lt; \) [[input:ans2]]<br></p><p>[[input:ans3]] \(\leq q&lt;\) [[input:ans4]]<br></p><p>[[input:ans5]] \(\leq r&lt;\) [[input:ans6]]</p><p>[[validation:ans1]][[validation:ans2]][[validation:ans3]][[validation:ans4]][[validation:ans5]][[validation:ans6]]</p><p style="text-align: right;"><b>(3)</b></p><p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(ii) Given that&nbsp;<br>\[ Q = \frac{p-q}{r} \]</p><p>Find the lower bound of \(Q\), giving your answer correct to 2 decimal places.</p><p>\(Q =\)[[input:ans7]]</p><p>[[validation:ans7]]<br></p><p>[[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p></p><p style="text-align: center;"><u><br></u></p><p style="text-align: center;"><u><h3>Worked Solution:</h3></u></p><p><u><br></u>All of the numbers are correct to 2 s.f. - this means they all behave a little differently.</p><p><br></p><ul><li>The second significant figure of {@aa@} is the {@(aa-100)/10@}, so we've rounded to the nearest \(10\):<br>we take \(5\) either side and \[{@l_aa@} \leq {@aa@} &lt; {@u_aa@} \]</li><li>The second significant figure of {@bb@} is actually the \(0\), and not the {@bb/10@}, so we've rounded to the nearest&nbsp;<i>unit</i>:<br>we take \(0.5\) either side and \[{@l_bb@} \leq {@bb@} &lt; {@u_bb@}\]</li><li>The second significant figure of {@cc@} is the {@significantfigures((cc-7)*10,1)@}, so we've rounded to the nearest \(0.1\):<br>we take \(0.05\) either side and \[{@l_cc@} \leq {@cc@} &lt; {@u_cc@}\]</li></ul></p>

<p>To find the lower bound of \(Q\) with the given \(p\), \(q\) and \(r\), you are expected to find the right combination of the upper or lower bounds of&nbsp;\(p\), \(q\) and \(r\) to give you the lowest possible value of \(Q\).</p><p>Given&nbsp;\( \displaystyle Q = \frac{p-q}{r} \),</p><p>We want to use the smallest possible value, take away as much as possible and divide by as much as possible.</p><p>so&nbsp;\( \displaystyle Q = \frac{L_p-U_q}{U_r} \), and in this case&nbsp;\[ Q = \frac{{@l_aa@}-{@u_bb@}}{{@u_cc@}} \approx {@tans1_rd@}&nbsp; (2d.p.)&nbsp; \]</p><br><p></p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
